C = [
("h1", "11. 1 Corinthians 14:33–36"),
("p", "Claim: the instruction that women keep silence in the churches limits one kind of speaking and not all speaking, since Paul has already assumed in the same letter that women pray and prophesy. The ground he gives is not noise or custom but obedience, “as also saith the law.” The volume reads the verses as the KJV has them and argues that the limit stands for the Body’s assembly."),
("sub", "11.1 The verses"),
("p", "“Let your women keep silence in the churches: for it is not permitted unto them to speak; but they are commanded to be under obedience, as also saith the law. And if they will learn any thing, let them ask their husbands at home: for it is a shame for women to speak in the church. What? came the word of God out from you? or came it unto you only?” (1 Corinthians 14:34–36). The setting is the meeting. The verses follow an order for tongues and prophets, and the next verse is Paul’s statement that what he writes is the commandments of the Lord (14:37)."),
("sub", "11.2 What the verses cannot mean"),
("p", "The verses cannot mean that no woman may speak at all, because Paul wrote three chapters earlier of the woman who “prayeth or prophesieth” (1 Corinthians 11:5) and of the care she is to take when she does. N. T. Wright draws the same line: “What the passage cannot possibly mean is that women had no part in leading public worship, speaking out loud of course as they did so.” Thomas Constable states the common conclusion of the commentaries: “To summarize, the most common view is that Paul forbade some form of inappropriate speech, but not all speech.” The volume agrees. The word translated “speak” is general, and it is used in the same chapter of tongues and of prophets. The context has to decide what kind of speaking is in view."),
("sub", "11.3 What the context points to"),
("p", "Two points in the context bear on it. The first is the instruction just before: “Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge” (1 Corinthians 14:29). Others in the meeting are to weigh what the prophets say. The second is the second half of the instruction itself: “if they will learn any thing, let them ask their husbands at home” (1 Corinthians 14:35). The women are not to ask questions in the meeting but at home. Constable reads the two together: “Now he qualified this by saying that the women should not do so vocally in the church meetings as the men could,” the “this” being the judging of the prophets, and concludes that “It is improper for a woman to speak in church meetings in the situation that Paul addressed in the context.” The volume’s reading is close to his. The silence is of the speaking by which the others judge the prophets and ask about what has been said. It is the speaking of questioning and weighing, and it is not the speaking of prayer or prophecy."),
("sub", "11.4 “as also saith the law”"),
("p", "The ground Paul states is that the women “are commanded to be under obedience, as also saith the law” (1 Corinthians 14:34). He does not name the passage. Constable takes it to refer to “woman’s subordination to the authoritative man in her family (Gen. 3:16),” and the verse he cites reads, “thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee” (Genesis 3:16). Paul in 1 Timothy 2:13 cites the creation: “Adam was first formed, then Eve,” and 1 Corinthians 11:8, 9 argues from the same creation. The volume does not name one verse, since Paul does not. It notes that the appeal is to what Scripture says of the first man and woman, which is not a local practice."),
("p", "The word “law” needs one remark. The volume holds, with the Digital Study on the Law’s jurisdiction (Study 59), that the Body is not put under the law of Moses. But Paul does not appeal to the law as the Body’s rule here. He appeals to it as Scripture that speaks, as he does elsewhere in the same letter: “Say I these things as a man? or saith not the law the same also? For it is written in the law of Moses, Thou shalt not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treadeth out the corn” (1 Corinthians 9:8, 9), and he adds, “For our sakes, no doubt, this is written” (1 Corinthians 9:10). The same chapter names Isaiah as “the law” (1 Corinthians 14:21). The appeal is to Scripture as a witness, and it is Paul who applies it."),
("sub", "11.5 The question of the text"),
("p", "Some Greek manuscripts place verses 34 and 35 after verse 40, and some scholars have judged them added. Constable records that C. K. Barrett and Gordon Fee hold this, and N. T. Wright says, “I am in two minds whether to agree with those who say this verse is a later and non-Pauline interpolation.” The volume reads the verses as the KJV has them, in the place the KJV has them, and notes that the argument of the volume does not rest on them alone. The instruction of 1 Timothy 2:11–14, read in chapter 12, stands in its own letter."),
("sub", "11.6 Another reading"),
("p", "Wright offers an explanation he finds the strongest of those on offer, and which he takes from Ken Bailey: that in the Middle East men and women sat apart and the women, not understanding the language of the service, talked among themselves until they were told to be quiet. Wright says that this “would fit extremely well” with Paul’s concern “for order and decency in the church’s worship.” The volume reports the reading and answers it from the verses. Paul does not give noise or language as his reason. He gives obedience and the law, and he adds that it is “a shame.” A reading in which the ground is disorder does not state the ground Paul states."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 14:34–36 read three ways, and what each leaves unexplained.", [
    ("Reading", "Who holds it, as read", "What it leaves unexplained"),
    ("The silence is of one kind of speaking, the judging of the prophets and the questioning", "Thomas Constable; the volume", "Why Paul says “the churches” and “shame” if the matter is only the judging of the prophets"),
    ("The silence is a response to disorder, the women talking among themselves", "N. T. Wright, from Ken Bailey", "Why Paul’s stated ground is “obedience, as also saith the law” and not disorder"),
    ("The verses are not Paul’s", "Barrett and Fee, as Constable records; Wright is in two minds", "Why the verses stand in the KJV’s text, in a letter that assumes women prophesy"),
  ], [0.34, 0.26, 0.40]),
("sub", "11.7 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The verses limit one kind of speaking; the commentaries agree that they do not forbid all speech.",
    "The kind is the judging of the prophets and the questioning in the meeting; prayer and prophecy are assumed in 11:5.",
    "The ground is “obedience, as also saith the law”; it is not noise or local custom.",
    "The volume reads the verses as the KJV has them, notes the question of the text, and does not rest the argument on these verses alone.",
  ]),
]
